                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "[h3] A few tips: [/h3]\nchoose any class you like - there are no class-based restrictions on skills or abilities in the game,\nbuy or scavenge for chicken meat - it’s cheap and very useful for mages,\ndon't invest points into Trading, Restoration, or Repair,\ndon't waste precious skill points on Summoning,\ndon't kill the dwarf’s pigs in the starting village,\nhand in completed quests as late as possible, only after completely clearing the area.\n\n[h3] Why it's worth playing today: [/h3]\n1. A hidden gem that blends Diablo's action with Ultima's freedom: Divine Divinity is far deeper than a standard Diablo clone. It's a massive, open-world cRPG that laid the foundational roots for Divinity: Original Sin.\n\n2. True Sandbox Freedom: Heavily inspired by Ultima VII. You can move items, manipulate the environment, and use exploding barrels to cheese your way through tough fights.\n\n3. Handcrafted World: There is zero procedural generation. Every single item, secret, and enemy in every dungeon is permanently hand-placed by developers.\n\n4. Action meets cRPG: It balances fast-paced combat with non-linear quests, though it focuses more on environmental freedom rather than heavy, multi-layered dialogue like Fallout 2.\n\nI recommend this game for anyone who wants to see where modern Larian Studios began.",
